__d(function(e,a,t,r,n,o,s){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science \u2014 Geography",textbook:"The Earth: Our Habitat \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:17,chapter_title:"Major Domains of the Earth",estimated_study_time_minutes:45,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Identify and name the four major domains of the Earth: Lithosphere, Hydrosphere, Atmosphere, and Biosphere","Explain what each domain consists of and give examples","Name and locate the seven continents and five oceans on a map","State the percentage of Earth's surface covered by land and water","Describe why Earth is called the 'Blue Planet'","Explain how the four domains interact to support life on Earth","Connect the domains to India's geography (Indian Ocean, Deccan Plateau, Western Ghats)","Use correct geographic vocabulary: lithosphere, hydrosphere, atmosphere, biosphere, continent, ocean"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Basic awareness that Earth has land and water","Familiarity with the names of a few countries and oceans from earlier classes","Understanding that air surrounds us and is essential for life","Awareness that living organisms exist on land, in water, and in air"],introduction:{why_important:"Understanding the four major domains of the Earth gives us a framework to understand how our planet works \u2014 how land, water, air, and life are all connected. This is the foundation for all Earth science, geography, and environmental studies.",real_life_relevance:"Every time you drink water (Hydrosphere), stand on the ground (Lithosphere), breathe air (Atmosphere), or see a tree or animal (Biosphere), you are interacting with one of Earth's four domains. Understanding these domains helps us understand why we need to protect nature.",karnataka_context:"Karnataka sits on the Deccan Plateau (Lithosphere), is bordered by the Arabian Sea (Hydrosphere), breathes the same atmosphere as the rest of the world, and its Western Ghats are one of the world's richest Biosphere hotspots. India operates two research stations in Antarctica \u2014 Maitri and Bharati \u2014 studying the Lithosphere and atmosphere at Earth's most extreme location. The Indian Ocean, named after India, is the third largest ocean in the Hydrosphere.",connection_to_previous_learning:"This chapter builds on students' awareness of land and water and introduces a scientific way to divide the entire Earth into four interacting systems \u2014 the foundation for all geography topics ahead."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"Lithosphere",role:"The solid land part of the Earth \u2014 continents, islands, and ocean floors \u2014 on which humans build civilisations, grow food, and extract minerals"},{name:"Hydrosphere",role:"All water on Earth \u2014 oceans, rivers, lakes, glaciers, and groundwater \u2014 covering 71% of Earth's surface and supporting all aquatic life"},{name:"Atmosphere",role:"The thin gaseous envelope of air surrounding the Earth \u2014 providing oxygen, regulating temperature, blocking harmful UV rays, and making weather and climate possible"},{name:"Biosphere",role:"The narrow zone where all life exists \u2014 the meeting point of Lithosphere, Hydrosphere, and Atmosphere \u2014 home to all plants, animals, fungi, and microbes"}],plot:["The chapter opens by asking students to look at a globe and notice the blue (water) and green/brown (land) areas.","It introduces the four domains of Earth \u2014 Lithosphere, Hydrosphere, Atmosphere, and Biosphere \u2014 as the four major systems that make up our planet.","It explores the Lithosphere by naming the seven continents (Asia, Africa, North America, South America, Antarctica, Europe, Australia) and ranking them by size.","It explores the Hydrosphere by naming the five oceans (Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Southern, Arctic) and noting that 71% of Earth's surface is water.","It explains the Atmosphere \u2014 Earth's protective air blanket of nitrogen (78%) and oxygen (21%).","It introduces the Biosphere as the overlap zone where the other three domains interact to make life possible.","The chapter connects India to each domain \u2014 the Deccan Plateau (Lithosphere), Indian Ocean (Hydrosphere), monsoon winds (Atmosphere), and the Western Ghats biodiversity hotspot (Biosphere).","It closes by explaining how all four domains interact \u2014 the water cycle, carbon cycle, and food chains all connect the four domains."],moral:"Earth's four domains \u2014 land, water, air, and life \u2014 are deeply interconnected. Damaging one domain (e.g., polluting the Hydrosphere or deforesting the Biosphere) harms all others. Our responsibility is to understand and protect all four."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"The Four Domains of the Earth",simple_explanation:"The Earth can be divided into four major parts: the solid land (Lithosphere), all the water (Hydrosphere), the surrounding air (Atmosphere), and the zone where life exists (Biosphere).",detailed_explanation:"The Lithosphere is the solid outer crust of the Earth, including continents and ocean floors \u2014 the rocky foundation of our planet. The Hydrosphere includes all water: oceans (saltwater), rivers, lakes, glaciers, and groundwater (freshwater). It covers about 71% of Earth's surface. The Atmosphere is the thin blanket of gases \u2014 mainly nitrogen (78%) and oxygen (21%) \u2014 held by gravity around Earth. It provides the air we breathe, regulates temperature, and blocks harmful ultraviolet radiation. The Biosphere is not a separate physical layer but an overlap zone \u2014 the thin belt where Lithosphere, Hydrosphere, and Atmosphere meet and interact in ways that support life. Every living organism on Earth is part of the Biosphere.",real_life_example:"When you stand on the ground (Lithosphere), drink water (Hydrosphere), breathe air (Atmosphere), and observe a plant or animal (Biosphere) \u2014 you are interacting with all four domains simultaneously. They are not separate worlds; they constantly influence each other.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka provides examples of all four domains: the Deccan Plateau is the Lithosphere on which Bengaluru, Mysuru, and most of Karnataka's cities sit; the Arabian Sea and rivers like Cauvery and Krishna are the Hydrosphere; the monsoon winds that bring Karnataka's rain are part of the Atmosphere; and the Western Ghats, a UNESCO World Heritage biodiversity hotspot, is an extraordinarily rich part of the Biosphere.",common_mistakes:"Students often think the four domains are completely separate layers. In reality, they overlap heavily. The ocean (Hydrosphere) sits on the ocean floor (Lithosphere). Fish live in water (Hydrosphere) but are part of the Biosphere. Clouds form in the Atmosphere but carry Hydrosphere water. The Biosphere exists across all three physical domains.",memory_tip:"LHAB: Lithosphere (Land), Hydrosphere (H\u2082O/water), Atmosphere (Air), Biosphere (Biology/life). Or remember: 'Lily Had A Butterfly' \u2014 a butterfly lives at the intersection of land, water, and air \u2014 exactly like the Biosphere."},{concept_name:"Continents and Oceans",simple_explanation:"Earth's Lithosphere and Hydrosphere are divided into seven large land masses (continents) and five oceans. Asia is the biggest continent; Australia is the smallest. The Pacific is the biggest ocean; the Arctic is the smallest.",detailed_explanation:"The seven continents in order of size: Asia (~44.6 million sq km), Africa (~30.3 million sq km), North America (~24.7 million sq km), South America (~17.8 million sq km), Antarctica (~14 million sq km), Europe (~10.5 million sq km), and Australia/Oceania (~7.7 million sq km). The five oceans in order of size: Pacific (largest \u2014 larger than all continents combined), Atlantic, Indian (third largest, named after India), Southern (Antarctic Ocean), and Arctic (smallest). Oceans cover 71% of Earth's surface. Only about 3% of all water on Earth is fresh water; 97% is saltwater in the oceans.",real_life_example:"When you look at a globe, the blue area (oceans) is far bigger than the brown-green area (land). This tells you immediately that Earth is a water-dominated planet. If you spin the globe and let it stop randomly, there is a 71% chance your finger will land on water, and only a 29% chance of landing on land.",karnataka_example:"India \u2014 and Karnataka \u2014 are located in Asia, the world's largest continent. The Indian Ocean (third largest ocean) is named after India, and Karnataka's western coast faces the Arabian Sea (part of the Indian Ocean). Understanding the relative sizes of oceans helps students appreciate why the Indian Ocean trade routes were so historically important \u2014 ships from Arabia, Africa, and Southeast Asia all crossed the same ocean to reach Indian shores.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes confuse continents with countries. A continent is a large landmass; a country is a political unit. Australia is both a continent and a country (unique). Greenland, despite being huge, is an island \u2014 not a continent. Also, Antarctica is a continent, not just a frozen ocean. Students may confuse the number of oceans \u2014 India's NCERT syllabus recognises 5 oceans (including the Southern/Antarctic Ocean).",memory_tip:"Continents (largest to smallest): 'A Friendly Neighbour Says All Europeans Are Awesome' \u2014 Asia, Africa, North America, South America, Antarctica, Europe, Australia. Oceans (largest to smallest): 'Powerful Athletes Incredibly Surpass All' \u2014 Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Southern, Arctic."},{concept_name:"The Atmosphere and Biosphere",simple_explanation:"The Atmosphere is Earth's protective air blanket made mostly of nitrogen and oxygen. The Biosphere is the narrow life zone where land, water, and air all meet and cooperate to support living organisms.",detailed_explanation:"The Atmosphere has several layers \u2014 the troposphere (where weather happens, closest to Earth), stratosphere (contains the ozone layer that blocks UV rays), mesosphere, thermosphere, and exosphere. For Grade 6, the key fact is its composition: 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, and 1% other gases including CO\u2082 and argon. The Atmosphere regulates Earth's temperature (without it, surface temperatures would swing from +120\xb0C by day to \u2212170\xb0C by night), enables the water cycle (evaporation and rainfall), and makes sound transmission possible. The Biosphere extends from about 10 km above sea level to 10 km below the ocean surface. It is not a uniform zone \u2014 life is most abundant at Earth's surface and in the shallow ocean (up to 200 m depth).",real_life_example:"The Atmosphere is why Earth has weather and a stable climate. Without the Atmosphere, there would be no monsoon \u2014 and without monsoon, Karnataka's agriculture would collapse. The Biosphere is why the Western Ghats teem with thousands of species of plants, insects, amphibians, birds, and mammals in every square kilometre \u2014 all of them living at the intersection of land (Lithosphere), rain (Hydrosphere), and air (Atmosphere).",karnataka_example:"Karnataka's Biosphere contributions are extraordinary: the Western Ghats in Karnataka alone have over 5,000 species of flowering plants, 450 species of birds, 140 species of mammals, and 250 species of butterflies. India's two Antarctic research stations \u2014 Maitri (1989) and Bharati (2012) \u2014 study the Atmosphere (climate and ozone research) and Lithosphere (ice core drilling) at the South Pole, showing how domains are studied globally.",common_mistakes:"Students often think the Biosphere is just the forests or jungles. In fact, the Biosphere includes life in oceans (fish, whales, coral), in the soil (earthworms, microbes), in rivers (fish, amphibians), on mountaintops (alpine plants, snow leopards), and even in the deep ocean trenches. Life exists virtually everywhere on Earth where there is liquid water and some energy source.",memory_tip:"Biosphere = Bio (life) + Sphere (zone). It's the 'life zone' \u2014 the only known zone in the entire universe where life exists. Protect it! The Atmosphere is Earth's invisible shield \u2014 punch a hole in it (ozone depletion) and harmful UV radiation reaches Earth, causing skin cancer and damaging crops."}],key_terms_glossary:[{term:"Lithosphere",meaning:"The solid outer layer of the Earth including the crust and upper mantle. It forms the land surface (continents and ocean floors) on which we live."},{term:"Hydrosphere",meaning:"All the water found on Earth \u2014 in oceans, seas, rivers, lakes, glaciers, and groundwater. It covers about 71% of Earth's surface."},{term:"Atmosphere",meaning:"The thin blanket of gases surrounding the Earth, held by gravity. It contains mainly nitrogen (78%) and oxygen (21%) and protects life by regulating temperature and blocking UV rays."},{term:"Biosphere",meaning:"The narrow zone of Earth where all life exists \u2014 the meeting zone of Lithosphere, Hydrosphere, and Atmosphere. All plants, animals, fungi, and microbes are part of the Biosphere."},{term:"Continent",meaning:"A large continuous landmass on Earth's surface. There are seven continents: Asia, Africa, North America, South America, Antarctica, Europe, and Australia."},{term:"Ocean",meaning:"A vast body of saltwater covering a large area of Earth's surface. There are five oceans: Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Southern, and Arctic."},{term:"Freshwater",meaning:"Water with very low salt content, suitable for drinking and agriculture. Only about 3% of Earth's total water is freshwater; the rest is saltwater in the oceans."},{term:"Nitrogen",meaning:"The most abundant gas in the Earth's atmosphere, making up 78% of air. It is essential for making proteins and is part of the nitrogen cycle in nature."},{term:"Oxygen",meaning:"The second most abundant gas in the atmosphere (21%). It is essential for animal respiration and combustion. Plants produce oxygen through photosynthesis."},{term:"Blue Planet",meaning:"A name for Earth, reflecting the fact that 71% of its surface is covered by water, giving it a predominantly blue appearance when viewed from space."}],worked_examples:[{example_number:1,type:"Classification",question:"Classify the following into the correct domain of the Earth: a river, a mountain, a thundercloud, a tiger, the ocean floor, and a fish.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Recall the four domains \u2014 Lithosphere (solid land), Hydrosphere (all water), Atmosphere (air and gases), Biosphere (all living organisms).","Step 2: A river \u2014 water = Hydrosphere.","Step 3: A mountain \u2014 solid land = Lithosphere.","Step 4: A thundercloud \u2014 clouds form in the Atmosphere (from water vapour \u2014 connecting Hydrosphere to Atmosphere).","Step 5: A tiger \u2014 a living organism = Biosphere.","Step 6: The ocean floor \u2014 solid rock under the ocean = Lithosphere (ocean floors are part of the crust).","Step 7: A fish \u2014 a living organism living in water = Biosphere (even though it lives in the Hydrosphere, as a living organism it belongs to the Biosphere).","Conclusion: River = Hydrosphere. Mountain = Lithosphere. Thundercloud = Atmosphere. Tiger = Biosphere. Ocean floor = Lithosphere. Fish = Biosphere."]},{example_number:2,type:"Comprehension",question:"Arrange the seven continents from largest to smallest and name one country in each.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Recall the mnemonic \u2014 'A Friendly Neighbour Says All Europeans Are Awesome'.","Step 2: Asia (largest) \u2014 India, China, Japan, Saudi Arabia.","Step 3: Africa \u2014 Nigeria, Egypt, Kenya, South Africa.","Step 4: North America \u2014 USA, Canada, Mexico.","Step 5: South America \u2014 Brazil, Argentina, Peru.","Step 6: Antarctica \u2014 no countries; only research stations (including India's Maitri and Bharati).","Step 7: Europe \u2014 France, Germany, UK, Italy.","Step 8: Australia (smallest) \u2014 Australia (the country shares the continent's name).","Conclusion: Asia \u2192 Africa \u2192 North America \u2192 South America \u2192 Antarctica \u2192 Europe \u2192 Australia."]},{example_number:3,type:"Application",question:"Why is it said that 'the four domains of the Earth are interdependent'? Give one real-life example from Karnataka.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Define interdependence \u2014 it means each domain depends on and influences the others.","Step 2: Give the Karnataka example \u2014 The Western Ghats (Lithosphere/mountains) force monsoon winds (Atmosphere) to rise and drop heavy rainfall (Hydrosphere) on the western slopes.","Step 3: Continue \u2014 This rainfall feeds rivers like the Cauvery (Hydrosphere) that nourish forests (Biosphere).","Step 4: Continue \u2014 The forests of the Biosphere absorb CO\u2082 and release O\u2082 into the Atmosphere, and their roots hold the soil of the Lithosphere, preventing erosion.","Step 5: Conclusion \u2014 Remove any one domain (e.g., deforest the Biosphere) and you affect all others: less rainfall (Atmosphere/Hydrosphere disrupted), more soil erosion (Lithosphere damaged), and loss of biodiversity (Biosphere collapses). The four domains are inseparable."]}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"The Four Domains of the Earth",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"Name the four major domains of the Earth.",a:"Lithosphere (solid land), Hydrosphere (water), Atmosphere (air/gases), and Biosphere (zone of life)."},{type:"Recall",q:"What percentage of Earth's surface is covered by water?",a:"About 71% of Earth's surface is covered by water."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Why is the Biosphere called the 'life zone'?",a:"The Biosphere is the only zone on Earth where life exists. It is called the 'life zone' because it is the overlap area where Lithosphere, Hydrosphere, and Atmosphere meet and provide conditions suitable for living organisms."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Which two gases make up most of the Atmosphere, and in what proportions?",a:"Nitrogen (78%) and Oxygen (21%) make up about 99% of the Atmosphere."},{type:"Application",q:"Give one example of how the Atmosphere and Hydrosphere interact.",a:"The Atmosphere causes evaporation of ocean water (Hydrosphere), which rises as water vapour, forms clouds in the Atmosphere, and falls back as rainfall \u2014 completing the water cycle. Monsoon rain in Karnataka is one example."}]}],key_takeaways:["The Earth has four major domains: Lithosphere (land), Hydrosphere (water), Atmosphere (air), and Biosphere (life zone).","The Lithosphere includes seven continents \u2014 Asia is the largest, Australia is the smallest.","The Hydrosphere includes five oceans \u2014 Pacific is the largest, Arctic is the smallest.","71% of Earth's surface is water (Hydrosphere); only 29% is land (Lithosphere) \u2014 hence Earth is called the 'Blue Planet'.","The Atmosphere is mainly nitrogen (78%) and oxygen (21%).","The Biosphere is the narrow zone where all life exists \u2014 the meeting point of the other three domains.","Only about 3% of Earth's water is freshwater; the rest is saltwater in oceans.","India's research stations in Antarctica (Maitri and Bharati) study the Atmosphere and Lithosphere at the South Pole.","Karnataka's Western Ghats are a globally significant part of Earth's Biosphere \u2014 a UNESCO World Heritage biodiversity hotspot.","The four domains are interdependent \u2014 damaging one affects all others."],mind_map:{root:"Major Domains of the Earth",branches:[{branch:"Lithosphere",sub:["7 Continents: Asia, Africa, N.America, S.America, Antarctica, Europe, Australia","Solid rock crust + upper mantle","Ocean floors also part of Lithosphere"]},{branch:"Hydrosphere",sub:["5 Oceans: Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Southern, Arctic","71% of Earth's surface is water","Only 3% is freshwater","Rivers, lakes, glaciers, groundwater included"]},{branch:"Atmosphere",sub:["78% Nitrogen + 21% Oxygen + 1% others","Protects from UV rays","Regulates temperature","Enables weather and climate"]},{branch:"Biosphere",sub:["Overlap of other 3 domains","All living organisms: plants, animals, microbes","Western Ghats = rich Biosphere hotspot","From 10 km above to 10 km below ocean"]},{branch:"Karnataka Connections",sub:["Deccan Plateau = Lithosphere","Arabian Sea = Hydrosphere","Monsoon winds = Atmosphere","Western Ghats = Biosphere hotspot","India's Maitri & Bharati stations in Antarctica"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 17 introduces the four major domains of the Earth. The Lithosphere is the solid land (crust + upper mantle), covering 29% of Earth's surface and including seven continents (Asia largest, Australia smallest). The Hydrosphere includes all water \u2014 five oceans (Pacific largest, Arctic smallest) and freshwater bodies \u2014 covering 71% of Earth's surface; 97% is saltwater, only 3% freshwater. The Atmosphere is Earth's gaseous envelope: 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, 1% other gases; it protects life, regulates climate, and enables weather. The Biosphere is the narrow life zone (overlap of the other three domains) extending from 10 km above to 10 km below sea level, home to all living organisms. Key facts: Earth = 'Blue Planet' (71% water). Continents mnemonic: 'A Friendly Neighbour Says All Europeans Are Awesome'. Oceans mnemonic: 'Powerful Athletes Incredibly Surpass All'. Karnataka connections: Deccan Plateau (Lithosphere), Arabian Sea (Hydrosphere), monsoon (Atmosphere), Western Ghats (Biosphere). India's Antarctic stations: Maitri (1989) and Bharati (2012).",must_remember_facts:["Four domains: Lithosphere (land), Hydrosphere (water), Atmosphere (air), Biosphere (life zone).","Seven continents: Asia (largest), Africa, North America, South America, Antarctica, Europe, Australia (smallest).","Five oceans: Pacific (largest), Atlantic, Indian, Southern, Arctic (smallest).","71% of Earth = water (Hydrosphere); 29% = land (Lithosphere).","Atmosphere: 78% Nitrogen + 21% Oxygen.","Only 3% of Earth's water is freshwater.","Earth is called the 'Blue Planet' because of its vast water coverage.","Antarctica has no permanent human population; India operates Maitri and Bharati research stations there.","Biosphere = the only known zone in the universe where life exists.","The Indian Ocean (third largest) is named after India."]}},2557,[]);